@packages/sigilc/vocabulary.sigil from SigilClaimsVocabulary import { AcceptedVocabulary, AtomDiscipline }
@packages/core/src/design-input.sigil from SigilDesignInput import { ResolvedDesignInput }

component SigilComputedClaims {
  goal {
    Decide the design findings that follow from an external interpretation of
    authored prose, and hand the rest to a judge as closed questions over
    computed facts.
  }

  interface {
    CompiledGuidance {
      Publish the claim vocabulary, the seven contract roles, one worked example
      per role, and the refusal cases to the external interpreter. Guidance is
      compiled into the binary and released with it; an operator may write an
      editable copy to a named path outside the workspace under validation.
      Fingerprint the guidance text, the vocabulary and the laws as one runtime
      identity. Guidance changes what the interpreter is told and never what the
      tool accepts.
    }

    InterpretationRequest {
      Project one design source and its resolved closure into an interpretation
      directory. Carry each Facet's exact prose slice with the contract role its
      unit belongs to, taken from ResolvedDesignInput rather than inferred, and
      emit one pre-filled row per Facet identifying that Facet alone. Write an
      immutable binding over the export digest, the selected source, the guidance
      fingerprint and the vocabulary generation. The caller keeps the directory
      and produces claims independently.

      Every Facet in the closure is presented, not only the selected source's.

      A request asks only for units whose stored interpretation is stale. A
      stored interpretation is keyed on the unit's prose, its owning component,
      its contract role, the guidance fingerprint and the vocabulary generation,
      never on the binding. A Logic unit is a whole section and is keyed on that
      section's prose; a unit in every other role is a Facet and is keyed on its
      own. A supplied interpretation of a unit that is not stale is a second
      interpretation of that unit. A reused interpretation is admitted afresh
      against the current closure, so a claim naming an entity that has since
      left it is refused like any other.

      Logic is the exception to presenting each Facet alone: a component's Logic
      Facets are presented together, in source order, each keeping its own row,
      identity and prose slice.
    }

    ReturnedClaims {
      Accept interpreted claims under AtomDiscipline. Relations and properties
      are closed over AcceptedVocabulary. Modality distinguishes required,
      permitted and assumed commitments, and an expectation records whether a
      claim asserts its relation holds or asserts it must not; a required claim
      expected not to hold is a prohibition. A reading row records a Facet the
      interpreter read and found no commitment in, or whose intent is
      unresolved. The tool mints every identity and refuses any the
      interpretation declares, and any entity outside the resolved closure.

      A component's Logic prose describing a sequence of steps is interpreted as
      one flow graph spanning that section's Facets.

      A step and a graph are entities this tool mints. The graph is returned as
      a row declaring each step and a row per guard a step applies; everything
      else is an ordinary claim about those entities — a state the step reads or
      writes, an operation it calls, an edge to a later step.

      A returned row names a step by its ordinal and the graph by its component
      and section, never by an identity. An ordinal is unique across the
      presented section, and a row naming one that no step declaration carries
      is refused.

      An edge runs from a step to whatever the prose describes as consuming what
      that step produced: a later step, or the flow's result. What a step reads,
      writes and calls are claims about that step rather than edge targets. An
      edge may cross from one Facet of the section to another. An edge to the
      graph is what declares an end, and a graph declaring no end is refused.

      A step row names the Facet it was authored in, and identity, grounding and
      the Facet's own interpretation all follow that naming. A Facet that
      contributed a step counts as interpreted. A step naming a Facet outside
      the presented section is refused.

      A flow step's guard compares a state read, an input value or a Constraints
      Facet; no other operand is accepted. An input value is text and is never
      resolved as an entity. Guarding on a Facet answers every obligation that
      Facet's claims raise against the graph.

      A step's and a graph's identities depend on nothing the interpretation
      chose but the ordinal, so two supplied orderings of one flow mint the
      same identities. A step that calls an already-declared Component or Tag
      operation names that operation as the object of a claim rather than
      taking its identity.

      A second interpretation of a unit, when the caller supplies one, is
      compared against the first: claims and flow-graph rows alike. Disagreement
      is reported as an observation about interpretation stability. The first
      interpretation remains the sole basis for findings, and the comparison
      gates no acceptance.

      Refusing any row refuses the whole supplied interpretation, never its
      valid portion.
    }

    SectionAwareClosure {
      Fill each claim's contract role from the unit it derives from, as
      ResolvedDesignInput attributes it, then derive holding relations,
      reachability, delegation, contradictions, ownership conflicts and
      obligations under finite tool-owned laws, inspecting absence only after
      the closure stabilizes. A claim whose role is Decisions is retained and
      reported but raises no obligation and fills none. An assumed claim raises
      an obligation to state what it leans on, and fills none.

      Two committing claims that cannot both hold contradict each other: they
      assert one proposition with opposite expectations, one asserts what
      another excludes, or they disagree about a property or a measure of one
      entity. State a claim marks exclusive, owned by two components, is an
      ownership conflict. These laws read no flow.

      Derive, for every flow graph, whether each step's edges reach one of the
      graph's declared ends. A branching flow declares one end per branch.

      Grounding asks whether the entity a row names could have come from the
      Facet that names it, and reads the kind of what it names: a Tag or
      Component against that Facet's resolved references, its owning component
      and its providers; a step against the Facet its declaration names; a graph
      against the section it is minted from. It applies to every returned row,
      including a guard's operands, at admission. A degenerate or ungrounded row
      in a graph suppresses that graph's reachability check, and both the row
      and the suppression are reported. An edge from a step to itself is a loop
      rather than a claim that says nothing.

      A claim whose role is Constraints reaches the flow graphs its own
      component owns and those of every component it transitively depends on,
      independently of grounding.

      A step in a graph a Constraints claim reaches is checked against that
      claim by what the step's own rows say it does. A step doing what the claim
      forbids contradicts it. A claim whose relation requires something of the
      graph, and whose Facet no step guards on, is unmet; a prohibition raises
      no obligation, because the contradiction above already answers it.

      A step writing state a claim marks exclusive, whose component does not own
      that state, is an ownership conflict.
    }

    ComputedFindings {
      Report contradictions, ownership conflicts and unmet obligations with the
      law that produced each one, with the claims it rests on where it rests on
      any, and with the steps and the graph where steps produced it. Report a
      claim whose subject and object coincide as degenerate, and one that
      grounding refuses as ungrounded; neither satisfies its unit. Name a Facet
      of the selected source in a declared role that yielded nothing — no
      interpretation satisfying its unit and no reading row — and attribute that
      gap to the interpretation.

      Report a flow-graph step with no path to one of its graph's declared ends
      as its own finding, naming the step and the graph it belongs to, whether
      or not a claim whose role is Constraints reaches that graph.

      A finding is reported by each run whose selected source authored a step it
      names or a Facet it names, so one spanning two sources reaches both
      authors and one spanning none is reported nowhere.

      An unchanged export, selected source, runtime identity, store and set of
      supplied interpretations produce an identical report.
    }

    JudgmentContext {
      Emit one entry per unit of the selected source, including units the
      closure found nothing about. Carry derived facts, obligation state and the
      provenance of each derived conclusion, and for every unfilled obligation
      the promise that raised it and where that promise is stated. Propose
      simplification candidates without ruling on them. Carry the prose an entry
      refers to, which for a Logic entry is every Facet of its section, so a
      consumer needs no access to the sources.
    }

    ClaimsCommands {
      Offer preparation, ingestion and guidance extraction as a separate
      executable beside the compiler. Refuse a binding that does not match the
      running invocation on any of its four parts. Exit zero for pass and
      warning, one for a gate failure, two for usage and a refused input, and
      three for operational failure. Only a contradiction or an ownership
      conflict the closure derives without reading a flow is a gate failure;
      every other finding, including one resting on the interpretation's reading
      of a flow, is a warning.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ClaimsCommands {
      Launch no model, worker or task workflow. The external interpretation is an
      input the caller supplies and re-supplies, never a call this executable
      makes. Reusing a stored interpretation is reuse of what a caller already
      supplied.
    }

    ReturnedClaims {
      Accepting a claim establishes neither that it is true of its Facet nor that
      the interpretation was faithful.
    }

    SectionAwareClosure {
      Workers supply no laws and fill no tool-owned table. The compiler's stored
      projections, its world cache and the files its runtime identity
      fingerprints are read-only to this component; writing any of them would
      invalidate every stored world. Findings and stores live under a path this
      component owns.
    }

    CompiledGuidance {
      Guidance discovered in the workspace under validation is refused. The test is
      provenance, not location.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ReturnedClaims {
      Extending identity minting to flow steps, instead of restricting
      decomposition to steps that call an already-declared operation, was chosen
      so decomposition covers the internal steps flow-shaped prose actually
      describes.

      A step and a graph are minted entity kinds rather than shapes of their own,
      so a flow fact is an ordinary claim. An earlier draft gave every flow fact
      its own row kind, on the reasoning that a claim's operands must be a
      declared Component or Tag. That reasoning was wrong: identity here was
      never only those two, and treating it that way turned seven ordinary facts
      into seven bespoke shapes and a join in every law.

      A step's identity is minted from the Facet its declaration names and the
      ordinal it carries; a graph's from its component and section. The ordinal
      is the one reference both sides can compute, because the interpretation
      cannot name a minted identity and the tool cannot read the prose.

      Flow-graph rows join the same repeat-interpretation comparison as claims
      rather than getting a stability mechanism of their own, because a graph is
      a harder extraction than a flat claim and carries the same risk: a
      nondeterministic reading presented as settled fact.

      A guard names a Constraints Facet rather than a claim because claim
      identities are minted after an interpretation returns, so a returned row
      could not carry one.
    }

    SectionAwareClosure {
      Facts are re-emitted from parsed values into a program this tool authors,
      so an interpretation is data the laws read rather than anything that runs.

      Contradiction and ownership conflict were drafted for steps, withdrawn and
      redefined. The withdrawn ownership draft fired on every cross-component
      call, because flows are written as delegation; reading a step's writes
      rather than its calls is what fixed it.

      The obligation law covers only claims that require something of the graph.
      Applied to prohibitions it double-reported with the contradiction law, and
      guarding on a prohibition is not a meaningful act.

      The ownership law requires a claim marking the state exclusive, because a
      component declaring state does not thereby say another may not write it.
      The claim-level ownership law needs the same marking for the same reason.
      Without it this tool would report a design that contradicts nothing. One
      foreign write is enough once the marking exists: exclusivity is what the
      marking asserts, so a second writer is not needed to contradict it.

      One bad row suppresses a whole graph's reachability check rather than being
      dropped, because dropping one edge would manufacture dead ends on
      well-formed steps upstream.

      A flow is expected to declare more than one end, because a returned
      result, an outward call and a terminating state write all end one and a
      branch may end each way. Each is declared by the same edge to the graph;
      none is inferred from what a step does.
    }

    ComputedFindings {
      A finding naming two sources is reported to both authors rather than routed
      to one, because neither author can fix it alone. An ownership conflict is
      the case that made this necessary: it names steps in two components by
      construction, so a rule routing it to one would always blind the other.
    }

    InterpretationRequest {
      A flow graph spans a section rather than a Facet because a Facet is a
      paragraph: projecting two real designs produced five and six separate Logic
      Facets, and in the five-Facet case the flow runs through three of them with
      the closing return in a Facet of its own. A Logic section mixes flow Facets
      with others, so the count is not the span. Scoping a graph to one Facet was
      rejected on that evidence — it would have left the common shape
      unexpressible while appearing to work on contrived single-paragraph flows.
      Scoping a graph to one grouping block instead was considered and not taken:
      the evidence cannot separate the two, because the flow observed sits in a
      single block. A component that names two Logic flows therefore yields one
      graph today, and that is the case to revisit if it appears.

      The request widened to the whole closure because every design source
      measured declares exactly one component, so a claim had no dependency
      graph to reach. Storing interpretations is what keeps that widening from
      re-reading every shared dependency once per dependent.
    }
  }

  cases {
    SectionAwareClosure {
      The same claim text raises an obligation from a Constraints Facet and none
      from a Decisions Facet. A permitted commitment raises no obligation where a
      required one does. A Facet in a declared role that yields only a reading row
      is interpreted, and one that yields nothing at all is a named gap. A Logic
      Facet that contributes no step but yields a claim is interpreted, not a gap.
      A step writing state another component declares is no ownership conflict;
      the same write is one where a claim marks that state exclusive.
    }

    ReturnedClaims {
      An artifact carrying one valid claim beside one rule declaration is refused
      whole. A claim relating a local Facet to a Tag imported from a source in the
      closure is accepted. A guard comparing an input value to a state read is
      accepted. An edge naming an ordinal no step declares is refused.
    }
  }
}
